### Is `auth.sessions` queryable?

**Yes, as a database table, but that does not mean it is exposed through the normal Data API.**

- The sessions guide says to **“check that the `session_id` claim in the JWT corresponds to a row in the `auth.sessions` table.”**
- Supabase’s OAuth token-security guide includes a SQL example joining `auth.sessions`: [https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/oauth-server/token-security](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/oauth-server/token-security). The example contains `LEFT JOIN auth.sessions s ON s.client_id = oc.client_id`.
- However, the User Management guide says: **“For security, the Auth schema is not exposed in the auto-generated API.”** [https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/managing-user-data](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/auth/managing-user-data)

So, you can use `auth.sessions` in database SQL, but don’t assume a client can select it through `supabase-js`/the auto-generated API or that a client role has direct table privileges. For RLS, reading `session_id` from the JWT is the straightforward part; checking whether that session row still exists is a separate database-access question.

### Built-in concurrent-session or device limit?

**Supabase offers a built-in single-session setting, but the default is unlimited active sessions.** The sessions guide says: **“By default, it lasts indefinitely and a user can have an unlimited number of active sessions on as many devices.”** It describes a **“Single session per user”** option that keeps the most recently active session; it also says the setting is available **“only on Pro Plans and up.”** The guide further says enforcement happens when a session is refreshed, not proactively at the moment settings change.

