{"kind":"tool","id":"XTYCeDoh0Qw","toolName":"exit_plan","input":{"plan":"# Multi-device limit — design and build plan

**Nothing is built yet.** This is the design, built from your four rulings.

---

## 1. The rule, in one line

A paid plan allows a fixed number of devices **at the same time** — **Starter 1, Pro 2, and one per member on all three chamber tiers, the owner included**. The number is enforced by the server, and a device over the limit is **signed out and emptied — but only after its unsent work has been handed to the account**, and **never while the account has no live plan**, because then there is nowhere to hand it to.

Today none of this is enforced: `payment_plans.dart` carries `deviceCap` with the note *"not enforced yet (user ruling, 2026-10-19)"*, and the paywall only states the number.

## 2. How a device is identified

Two things are needed, and only one of them can be trusted.

| Role | What it is | Why |
|---|---|---|
| **Enforcement key** | the **session id** inside the access token (`auth.jwt() ->> 'session_id'`) | Server-issued, one per sign-in, impossible for a client to forge. Supabase documents this claim as always present in its JWTs. It is what the server counts. |
| **Convenience key** | a **device key**: Android's SSAID, or a stored UUID on Windows | So signing out and back in on the same phone **reuses its own slot** instead of consuming another. |
| **Display only** | brand + model, platform, last contact | For the list the lawyer reads. |

The device key is client-supplied, so it is never the thing  [… 7,458 characters omitted] -day flush window, the `device_is_active()` clause on the read policies, token deletion on revoke. The 16 SQL proofs that set JWT claims gain a `session_id` in their helper; every one must still pass.

**Phase 3 — the client registers and explains.** Device key + label, registration at launch and before each sync, `SyncHealthStatus.deviceBlocked`, the refusal dialog with the device list, and a **Devices** screen under Manage Account (label, platform, last used, Remove).

**Phase 4 — the revoked device.** Generalise `membership_revoked_scope` into a revoked-device gate: detect → push-only sync → `ack_device_wipe` → wipe → sign out → explanation screen, including "N changes saved" or "N changes could not be saved".

**Phase 5 — lapse, renewal and the ceiling falling.** No erase while unentitled; on renewal the N most recent keep their slots and the rest flush-and-erase; banner wording for both sides.

**Phase 6 — rollout and records.** The grandfather date, Terms of Service in English and Bengali (the device limit, and that an over-limit device is signed out and its local copy erased after its work is saved), the plan's payment-enforcement section, Task 21's phase record, session notes. No mockup is required for a screen that has none.

## 7. What cannot be enforced, stated plainly

- A phone that never comes back online keeps its local copy until it is next opened; nothing can erase it remotely.
- A revoked device may still receive a Realtime frame or two before it drops its channel.
- The auth session is not revoked at the identity server, so its token keeps refreshing; every table refuses it, but the session exists.
- A tampered client can choose its own device key; it cannot choose its session, so the cap holds.

## 8. Acceptance (checked at the end, per item)

1. Pro holds 2 devices; a third with an idle spare evicts the spare; a third when all are active is refused with the list.
2. Starter holds 1; chambers hold 1 per member, owner included.
3. The evicted device saves its unsent work, erases, and signs out — in that order, proven by tests on the order.
4. Nothing is erased while the account has no live plan; a lapse leaves every local diary intact.
5. Write and read gates both refuse a revoked session; service-role paths are unaffected.
6. Every existing SQL proof and the full Flutter suite still pass.
"}}
