ser request)** its label is now **Subscription Plans** (key `settingsManageAccount_purchaseNewSubscription` kept). Same icons and wording as the rows had in Manage Account. It is a sub-page rather than a bottom sheet because it is reference material a user reads — a plan, an expiry and a ledger they reconcile against a bank or MFS statement — not a choice they are making, and the ledger needs room the sheet never had. Two rules the page holds to: **an entitlement that has not landed reads a dash, never *Free*** (the entitlement bullet in §Payment Enforcement below, since a premium user whose profile pull is in flight must not read Free beside an expiry that names a date), and **every timestamp is Bangladesh civil time** — the date and the clock come from the same shifted instant, because a date formatted in UTC beside a clock formatted locally disagrees by six hours and the user reconciles the wrong moment. **No `settings_subscription` mockup exists**, so this is a recorded departure: the body is the two rows above moved verbatim, and it is shown to every account because the rows it replaces were unconditional and a premium user still needs to see what they paid for. It takes the icon **Go Premium** occupied, so the entry a free-plan user tapped keeps its look in its new home. **(0.0.442)** On a **Free** plan the page hides the **Expiry** row — nobody has an expiry when there is nothing to expire — and, before Payment History, shows a **Free Plan Limitations** section: the case ceiling from `freeTierCaseLimit`, and the cloud features a free account does not get (real-time backup, another device, sharing with associates) with their consequence (no cloud copy to restore a lost phone from). It is shown for a **known** Free plan only: a dash is neither Free nor premium, so an entitlement still in flight keeps its Expiry row and shows no limitations. **(0.0.452, user request)** The **Free Plan Limitations** heading is a plain title — the leading red bullet it used to carry is gone — and the three limitation bullets are **black** (`AppColors.secondary`) rather than error red. **(0.0.625, user request)** The three bullets carry the new wording — *Manage up to 50 cases at a time*, the cloud-feature sentence naming up to 20 associates, and *No cloud backup* — with the case count, the *No cloud backup:* lead and the offer's rates in bold. Below them the Free plan now shows **Want to Upgrade?** with the Starter per-month rates (3 months ৳60, 6 months ৳50, 1 year ৳45 — the last is why it says *starting at*) and a centred **Subscription Plans** button that opens the paywall. **(0.0.543, user request) A live paid plan can be cancelled from this page, and the ledger it leaves behind is Subscription History.** While a paid period is running and the plan is the account's own, the summary card carries a **Cancel** action at its right; a chamber member never sees it (their entitlement is the chamber's, and the RPC answers `firm_member`), and a free or lapsed plan has nothing to end. The confirmation window states plainly that a cancelled plan cannot be restored, with **Go Back** in black and **Confirm Cancellation** in red; confirming calls the server's `cancel_subscription()` RPC — the only writer of `subscription_cancellations` — which backdates the expiry behind the 48-hour grace and keeps the stored tier, then pulls the profile so the page reads **Pro — Lapsed** and shows its lapse allowance. The section formerly called **Payment History** is now **Subscription History**: `payment_submissions` and `subscription_cancellations` are read together under RLS, newest first and numbered as one list, and a cancellation is a card of its own carrying the plan and amount that were running, a red **Cancelled** label and the moment it happened. **(0.0.544, user request)** The **Cancel** action on the card is itself a filled red button with white text — the same red as the window's confirmation — rather than red text alone. **(0.0.545, user request)** The