Done — the name now travels inside the delete request itself, and it's live on the project.

**What the owner sees now (the point of the change).** Open a member's deletion request and the line under the record says the real name — *Requested by: Jannatul Ferdousi Rekha* — immediately, while offline, and on the very first review. Before this, that line could only read the one profile row your own phone holds (your own), so it fell back to *A Firm Member* exactly when you needed to know who asked. The fix you asked for is exactly the reasoning you gave: a request you can't put a face to protects nobody.

**Why the phone doesn't get to write that name.** The name is filled in by the **server** from the requester's own profile, on every insert and update, so a tampered app cannot put somebody else's name on a request. The phone's own value stands only until that person's profile has a name at all (a brand-new account), which is better than a blank. That answers the trust caveat I raised against this option, rather than ignoring it.

**What changed.** A new column `requester_name` on `delete_requests` with a backfill and that deriving trigger ([20261026140000_delete_requests_requester_name.sql](supabase/migrations/20261026140000_delete_requests_requester_name.sql)); the app writes the requester's own name when the request is raised, through [delete_request_flow.dart](lib/features/settings/presentation/delete_request_flow.dart) → [delete_requests_repository.dart](lib/features/settings/data/delete_requests_repository.dart); the review screen prefers the request's own name ([delete_request_review_screen.dart](lib/features/settings/presentation/delete_request_review_screen.dart)) and keeps the old lookup only for requests written before this change; the local database went 14 → 15 (additive, nullable, nothing rewritten). The SQL ledger was regenerated and the guard counts moved with it.

**Verified on the live project, read-only.** The column and the trigger exist; the one request already in the queue was backfilled to its requester's name (zero unnamed). I then proved the trigger *derives* rather than trusts: a probe insert whose payload said *Name The Client Sent* stored **Zobayer Hasan** — the profile's own name — and because the probe raised an error inside its own transaction it was rolled back; the table went back to 1 request and 0 probe rows. So the check left nothing behind.

**Could this have been a policy change needing your approval?** No — and you were right to call that out. It was a display detail inside one chamber's own audit trail, not a change to who may see what: the request already names the member by id, the roster already lists everyone by name, and the server already restricts a request to that firm. Nothing about access changed; only whether the name is readable on the reviewer's phone.

